const e="jkssb-faa-weekly-mtcz05ia",t="jkssb-faa",i="JKSSB FAA Science Weekly Mini Mock",c=30,d=30,u=20,h=.25,a="2026-08-28T07:14:00+00:00",o="2026-09-01T07:14:00+00:00",n="2026-09-02T07:14:00+00:00",r="published",s=[{pyq:"JKSSB FAA 2024",tags:["Newtons Laws Of Motion"],source:"Subi Malik",status:"published",options:[{id:"a",text:"1 and 2 only",label:"A"},{id:"b",text:"2 and 3 only",label:"B"},{id:"c",text:"1 and 3 only",label:"C"},{id:"d",text:"1, 2, and 3",label:"D"}],question:"Consider the following statements regarding Newton's Laws of Motion:<br>1. First Law of Motion introduces the concept of inertia and defines force qualitatively.<br>2. Second Law of Motion provides a quantitative measure of force ($F = ma$) and states that the rate of change of momentum is proportional to the applied force.<br>3. Third Law of Motion states that action and reaction forces act on the same body simultaneously.<br>Which of the statements given above are correct?",correct_id:"a",difficulty:"medium",category_id:"general-science",explanation:"Statements 1 and 2 are correct. Statement 3 is incorrect because action and reaction forces always act on two different bodies simultaneously, which is why they equal and opposite but do not cancel each other out."},{pyq:null,tags:["Newtons Laws Of Motion"],source:"Subi Malik",status:"published",options:[{id:"a",text:"Increasing the impact time to decrease the rate of change of momentum and reduce force on hands",label:"A"},{id:"b",text:"Decreasing the impact time to increase the momentum of the ball",label:"B"},{id:"c",text:"Increasing the impulse by applying a larger force over a shorter duration",label:"C"},{id:"d",text:"Cancelling the action-reaction pair according to Newton's Third Law",label:"D"}],question:"A cricket player pulls his hands backward while catching a fast-moving cricket ball. Which scientific principle explains this action?",correct_id:"a",difficulty:"easy",category_id:"general-science",explanation:"Pulling the hands backward increases the impact time ($\\Delta t$). According to Newton's Second Law ($F = \\frac{\\Delta p}{\\Delta t}$), increasing the time interval decreases the rate of change of momentum, thereby reducing the force exerted on the player's hands to prevent injury."},{pyq:"JKSSB FAA 2024",tags:["Mass Weight Gravitation"],source:"Subi Malik",status:"published",options:[{id:"a",text:"1 and 2 only",label:"A"},{id:"b",text:"2 and 3 only",label:"B"},{id:"c",text:"1 and 3 only",label:"C"},{id:"d",text:"1, 2, and 3",label:"D"}],question:"Consider the following statements regarding Acceleration due to Gravity ($g$):<br>1. The value of $g$ is maximum at the poles and minimum at the equator due to Earth's shape and rotation.<br>2. The value of $g$ becomes zero at the center of the Earth.<br>3. Acceleration due to gravity depends directly on the mass of the freely falling object.<br>Which of the statements given above are correct?",correct_id:"a",difficulty:"medium",category_id:"general-science",explanation:"Statements 1 and 2 are correct. Statement 3 is incorrect because acceleration due to gravity is given by $g = \\frac{GM}{R^2}$, where $M$ is the mass of the Earth; $g$ is independent of the mass ($m$) of the falling object."},{pyq:null,tags:["Mass Weight Gravitation"],source:"Subi Malik",status:"published",options:[{id:"a",text:"Mass = $60\\text{ kg}$, Weight = $100\\text{ N}$",label:"A"},{id:"b",text:"Mass = $10\\text{ kg}$, Weight = $100\\text{ N}$",label:"B"},{id:"c",text:"Mass = $60\\text{ kg}$, Weight = $600\\text{ N}$",label:"C"},{id:"d",text:"Mass = $10\\text{ kg}$, Weight = $600\\text{ N}$",label:"D"}],question:"An object has a mass of $60\\text{ kg}$ on the surface of the Earth. What will be its mass and approximate weight on the surface of the Moon? (Take $g_{\\text{earth}} = 10\\text{ m/s}^2$)",correct_id:"a",difficulty:"medium",category_id:"general-science",explanation:"Mass is an intrinsic property that remains constant ($60\\text{ kg}$) anywhere in the universe. Weight on Earth is $W_e = m \\cdot g = 60 \\times 10 = 600\\text{ N}$. Since Moon's surface gravity is $\\frac{1}{6}\\text{th}$ of Earth's gravity, Weight on Moon is $\\frac{600}{6} = 100\\text{ N}$."},{pyq:"JKSSB FAA 2024",tags:["Work Energy Power"],source:"Subi Malik",status:"published",options:[{id:"a",text:"1 and 3 only",label:"A"},{id:"b",text:"2 and 3 only",label:"B"},{id:"c",text:"1 and 2 only",label:"C"},{id:"d",text:"1, 2, and 3",label:"D"}],question:"Which of the following scenarios results in ZERO work done by the applied force?<br>1. A satellite revolving around the Earth in a circular orbit under gravitational force.<br>2. A coolie carrying a load on his head and moving horizontally on a smooth platform.<br>3. A person pushing a rigid brick wall with maximum physical effort without moving it.<br>Select the correct code:",correct_id:"d",difficulty:"hard",category_id:"general-science",explanation:"Work done is $W = F d \\cos\\theta$. In scenario 1, gravitational force is perpendicular to displacement ($\\theta = 90^\\circ$). In scenario 2, vertical normal force/weight is perpendicular to horizontal displacement ($\\theta = 90^\\circ$). In scenario 3, displacement $d = 0$. Thus, work done is zero in all three cases."},{pyq:null,tags:["Electric Current"],source:"Subi Malik",status:"published",options:[{id:"a",text:"Remains unchanged",label:"A"},{id:"b",text:"Doubles (2 times)",label:"B"},{id:"c",text:"Quadruples (4 times)",label:"C"},{id:"d",text:"Reduces to one-fourth ($\\frac{1}{4}\\text{th}$)",label:"D"}],question:"If the length of a uniform metallic conducting wire is doubled and its area of cross-section is halved, how does its electrical resistance change?",correct_id:"c",difficulty:"medium",category_id:"general-science",explanation:"Electrical resistance is given by $R = \\rho \\frac{l}{A}$. When length becomes $2l$ and area becomes $\\frac{A}{2}$, the new resistance $R' = \\rho \\frac{2l}{A/2} = 4 \\left(\\rho \\frac{l}{A}\\right) = 4R$. Thus, resistance quadruples."},{pyq:null,tags:["Electric Current"],source:"Subi Malik",status:"published",options:[{id:"a",text:"$1\\text{ A}$",label:"A"},{id:"b",text:"$3\\text{ A}$",label:"B"},{id:"c",text:"$4.5\\text{ A}$",label:"C"},{id:"d",text:"$18\\text{ A}$",label:"D"}],question:"Two resistors of resistances $6\\,\\Omega$ and $12\\,\\Omega$ are connected in parallel across a battery of potential difference $12\\text{ V}$. What is the total electric current drawn from the battery?",correct_id:"b",difficulty:"medium",category_id:"general-science",explanation:"For resistors in parallel, $\\frac{1}{R_p} = \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{12} = \\frac{3}{12} \\implies R_p = 4\\,\\Omega$. Using Ohm's Law ($I = \\frac{V}{R_p}$), total current $I = \\frac{12\\text{ V}}{4\\,\\Omega} = 3\\text{ A}$."},{pyq:"JKSSB FAA 2024",tags:["Electric Current"],source:"Subi Malik",status:"published",options:[{id:"a",text:"1 and 2 only",label:"A"},{id:"b",text:"2 and 3 only",label:"B"},{id:"c",text:"1 and 3 only",label:"C"},{id:"d",text:"1, 2, and 3",label:"D"}],question:"Consider the following statements regarding an Electric Fuse wire used in domestic circuits:<br>1. It is connected in series with the live wire.<br>2. It must possess high electrical resistance and a low melting point.<br>3. It operates on the chemical effect of electric current.<br>Which of the statements given above are correct?",correct_id:"a",difficulty:"medium",category_id:"general-science",explanation:"Statements 1 and 2 are correct because a fuse protects domestic appliances by melting when excess current flows through it due to Joule's heating effect ($H = I^2 R t$). Statement 3 is incorrect because an electric fuse works on the heating effect of electric current, not the chemical effect."},{pyq:null,tags:["Electric Current"],source:"Subi Malik",status:"published",options:[{id:"a",text:"$3.6 \\times 10^3\\text{ Joules}$",label:"A"},{id:"b",text:"$3.6 \\times 10^5\\text{ Joules}$",label:"B"},{id:"c",text:"$3.6 \\times 10^6\\text{ Joules}$",label:"C"},{id:"d",text:"$3.6 \\times 10^8\\text{ Joules}$",label:"D"}],question:"The commercial unit of electrical energy consumed in households is Kilowatt-hour ($\\text{kWh}$). One kilowatt-hour is equal to:",correct_id:"c",difficulty:"easy",category_id:"general-science",explanation:"$1\\text{ kWh} = 1\\text{ kW} \\times 1\\text{ hour} = 1000\\text{ W} \\times 3600\\text{ seconds} = 3.6 \\times 10^6\\text{ Joules}$ (or $3.6\\text{ MJ}$)."},{pyq:null,tags:["Chemical Equations"],source:"Subi Malik",status:"published",options:[{id:"a",text:"$\\text{Fe} + \\text{H}_2\\text{O} \\rightarrow \\text{Fe}_3\\text{O}_4 + \\text{H}_2$",label:"A"},{id:"b",text:"$3\\text{Fe(s)} + 4\\text{H}_2\\text{O(g)} \\rightarrow \\text{Fe}_3\\text{O}_4\\text{(s)} + 4\\text{H}_2\\text{(g)}$",label:"B"},{id:"c",text:"$2\\text{Fe(s)} + 3\\text{H}_2\\text{O(g)} \\rightarrow \\text{Fe}_2\\text{O}_3\\text{(s)} + 3\\text{H}_2\\text{(g)}$",label:"C"},{id:"d",text:"$3\\text{Fe(s)} + 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O(g)} \\rightarrow \\text{Fe}_3\\text{O}_2\\text{(s)} + 2\\text{H}_2\\text{(g)}$",label:"D"}],question:"Which of the following balanced chemical equations correctly represents the Law of Conservation of Mass for the reaction between iron and steam?",correct_id:"b",difficulty:"medium",category_id:"general-science",explanation:"Option B is the correctly balanced NCERT chemical equation: 3 iron, 8 hydrogen, and 4 oxygen atoms are present on both LHS and RHS. Water in steam phase is designated as $\\text{H}_2\\text{O(g)}$."},{pyq:"JKSSB FAA 2024",tags:["Chemical Equations"],source:"Subi Malik",status:"published",options:[{id:"a",text:"X = $\\text{CaCO}_3$, Y = $\\text{CaO}$, Z = $\\text{O}_2$, W = $\\text{Ca(OH)}_2$",label:"A"},{id:"b",text:"X = $\\text{CaO}$, Y = $\\text{Ca(OH)}_2$, Z = $\\text{CO}_2$, W = $\\text{CaCO}_3$",label:"B"},{id:"c",text:"X = $\\text{Ca(OH)}_2$, Y = $\\text{CaO}$, Z = $\\text{CO}_2$, W = $\\text{CaSO}_4$",label:"C"},{id:"d",text:"X = $\\text{CaO}$, Y = $\\text{CaCO}_3$, Z = $\\text{N}_2$, W = $\\text{Ca(OH)}_2$",label:"D"}],question:"A solution of substance 'X' is used for whitewashing interior walls. Substance 'X' reacts vigorously with water to form 'Y', releasing a large amount of heat. After applying 'Y' on walls, it reacts slowly with gas 'Z' in air to form a shiny white layer of 'W'. Identify X, Y, Z, and W:",correct_id:"b",difficulty:"hard",category_id:"general-science",explanation:"Quicklime ($\\text{CaO}$, X) reacts with water to yield slaked lime ($\\text{Ca(OH)}_2$, Y) in an exothermic reaction. Painted slaked lime reacts with atmospheric carbon dioxide ($\\text{CO}_2$, Z) over 2-3 days to form a lustrous layer of calcium carbonate ($\\text{CaCO}_3$, W)."},{pyq:null,tags:["Chemical Equations"],source:"Subi Malik",status:"published",options:[{id:"a",text:"1 and 2 only",label:"A"},{id:"b",text:"2 and 3 only",label:"B"},{id:"c",text:"1 and 3 only",label:"C"},{id:"d",text:"1, 2, and 3",label:"D"}],question:"Consider the following statements regarding the electrolytic decomposition of water:<br>1. Hydrogen gas is evolved at the cathode (-ve electrode) while Oxygen gas is evolved at the anode (+ve electrode).<br>2. The volume of gas collected at the cathode is double the volume of gas collected at the anode.<br>3. Electrolysis of water is an exothermic reaction because electric current is supplied.<br>Which of the statements given above are correct?",correct_id:"a",difficulty:"hard",category_id:"general-science",explanation:"Statements 1 and 2 are correct because water decomposes into $\\text{H}_2$ (cathode) and $\\text{O}_2$ (anode) in a $2:1$ volume ratio ($2\\text{H}_2\\text{O} \\rightarrow 2\\text{H}_2 + \\text{O}_2$). Statement 3 is incorrect because all decomposition reactions requiring external energy input (electric, heat, or light) are endothermic."},{pyq:null,tags:["Chemical Equations"],source:"Subi Malik",status:"published",options:[{id:"a",text:"1 and 2 only",label:"A"},{id:"b",text:"2 and 3 only",label:"B"},{id:"c",text:"1 and 3 only",label:"C"},{id:"d",text:"1, 2, and 3",label:"D"}],question:"When an iron nail is dipped into an aqueous solution of Copper Sulphate ($\\text{CuSO}_4$), what visual changes are observed after some time?<br>1. The blue color of the copper sulphate solution fades and changes to light green.<br>2. A reddish-brown coating of copper metal gets deposited on the iron nail.<br>3. Iron displaces copper because iron is placed above copper in the reactivity series.<br>Which of the observations given above are correct?",correct_id:"d",difficulty:"medium",category_id:"general-science",explanation:"Iron is more reactive than copper ($\\text{Fe} &gt; \\text{Cu}$). It displaces copper from $\\text{CuSO}_4$ (blue) to form $\\text{FeSO}_4$ (light green) and deposits reddish-brown copper on the iron nail ($\\text{Fe} + \\text{CuSO}_4 \\rightarrow \\text{FeSO}_4 + \\text{Cu}$)."},{pyq:null,tags:["Chemical Equations"],source:"Subi Malik",status:"published",options:[{id:"a",text:"$\\text{CuO}$ is oxidized; $\\text{H}_2$ is the oxidizing agent",label:"A"},{id:"b",text:"$\\text{H}_2$ is oxidized; $\\text{CuO}$ is the oxidizing agent",label:"B"},{id:"c",text:"$\\text{CuO}$ is reduced; $\\text{CuO}$ is the reducing agent",label:"C"},{id:"d",text:"$\\text{H}_2$ is reduced; $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$ is the oxidizing agent",label:"D"}],question:"In the redox reaction: $\\text{CuO(s)} + \\text{H}_2\\text{(g)} \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} \\text{Cu(s)} + \\text{H}_2\\text{O(l)}$, which substance is oxidized and which acts as the oxidizing agent?",correct_id:"b",difficulty:"medium",category_id:"general-science",explanation:"Hydrogen ($\\text{H}_2$) gains oxygen to form $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$ and is therefore oxidized. Copper Oxide ($\\text{CuO}$) loses oxygen (reduced to $\\text{Cu}$) and acts as the oxidizing agent by supplying oxygen."},{pyq:"JKSSB FAA 2024",tags:["Chemical Equations"],source:"Subi Malik",status:"published",options:[{id:"a",text:"1 and 2 only",label:"A"},{id:"b",text:"2 and 3 only",label:"B"},{id:"c",text:"1 and 3 only",label:"C"},{id:"d",text:"1, 2, and 3",label:"D"}],question:"Which of the following pairings of chemical phenomena with their correct observation/prevention is/are CORRECT?<br>1. Black coating on tarnished Silver $\\rightarrow$ Silver Sulphide ($\\text{Ag}_2\\text{S}$)<br>2. Green coating on corroded Copper $\\rightarrow$ Basic Copper Carbonate ($\\text{CuCO}_3 \\cdot \\text{Cu(OH)}_2$)<br>3. Flushing of potato chips packets $\\rightarrow$ Inert Nitrogen gas ($\\text{N}_2$) to prevent oil oxidation<br>Select the correct code:",correct_id:"d",difficulty:"medium",category_id:"general-science",explanation:"All three are standard NCERT observations. Silver reacts with atmospheric $\\text{H}_2\\text{S}$ to form black $\\text{Ag}_2\\text{S}$. Copper reacts with moist $\\text{CO}_2$ to form green basic copper carbonate. Chips bags are flushed with $\\text{N}_2$ gas to prevent rancidity."},{pyq:null,tags:["Structure Of Atom"],source:"Subi Malik",status:"published",options:[{id:"a",text:"p-1, q-2, r-3, s-4",label:"A"},{id:"b",text:"p-2, q-1, r-4, s-3",label:"B"},{id:"c",text:"p-1, q-3, r-2, s-4",label:"C"},{id:"d",text:"p-4, q-2, r-3, s-1",label:"D"}],question:"Match Column-I (Subatomic Particle / Atomic Model) with Column-II (Discoverer / Scientist):<br>Column-I:<br>p. Electron<br>q. Atomic Nucleus<br>r. Neutron<br>s. Discrete Orbits (Quantized Energy Levels)<br>Column-II:<br>1. J.J. Thomson<br>2. Ernest Rutherford<br>3. James Chadwick<br>4. Niels Bohr<br>Select the correct matching code:",correct_id:"a",difficulty:"easy",category_id:"general-science",explanation:"J.J. Thomson discovered the electron (1897); Rutherford discovered the atomic nucleus through gold-foil alpha particle scattering (1911); James Chadwick discovered the neutron (1932); Niels Bohr proposed quantized shell orbits."},{pyq:null,tags:["Structure Of Atom"],source:"Subi Malik",status:"published",options:[{id:"a",text:"1 and 2 only",label:"A"},{id:"b",text:"2 and 3 only",label:"B"},{id:"c",text:"1 and 3 only",label:"C"},{id:"d",text:"1, 2, and 3",label:"D"}],question:"An atom of an element $X$ has an atomic number of $17$ and a mass number of $35$. Which of the following statements regarding element $X$ is/are correct?<br>1. It contains 17 protons, 17 electrons, and 18 neutrons.<br>2. Its electronic configuration is $2, 8, 7$ and its valency is $1$.<br>3. Element $X$ is Chlorine, a non-metal belonging to the halogen group.<br>Select the correct code:",correct_id:"d",difficulty:"medium",category_id:"general-science",explanation:"Atomic number $Z = 17$ (Protons = Electrons = 17). Neutrons = Mass Number ($A$) - $Z = 35 - 17 = 18$. Shell distribution: K=2, L=8, M=7. To achieve a stable octet, it gains 1 electron, giving a valency of 1. Element 17 is Chlorine."},{pyq:"JKSSB FAA 2024",tags:["Structure Of Atom"],source:"Subi Malik",status:"published",options:[{id:"a",text:"1 and 2 only",label:"A"},{id:"b",text:"2 and 3 only",label:"B"},{id:"c",text:"1 and 3 only",label:"C"},{id:"d",text:"1, 2, and 3",label:"D"}],question:"Consider the following statements regarding Isotopes and Isobars:<br>1. Isotopes have the same atomic number but different mass numbers due to varying numbers of neutrons.<br>2. Isobars have the same mass number but different atomic numbers (e.g., $^{40}_{18}\\text{Ar}$ and $^{40}_{20}\\text{Ca}$).<br>3. Cobalt-60 isotope is used in the treatment of goitre, while Iodine-131 isotope is used in cancer therapy.<br>Which of the statements given above is/are correct?",correct_id:"a",difficulty:"hard",category_id:"general-science",explanation:"Statements 1 and 2 are correct. Statement 3 is incorrect because Cobalt-60 is used in cancer radiation therapy, whereas Iodine-131 is used in treating goitre (thyroid disorders). The applications are interchanged."},{pyq:null,tags:["Life Processes"],source:"Subi Malik",status:"published",options:[{id:"a",text:"Absorption of light energy by chlorophyll",label:"A"},{id:"b",text:"Conversion of light energy into chemical energy and splitting of water into hydrogen and oxygen",label:"B"},{id:"c",text:"Reduction of carbon dioxide into carbohydrates (glucose)",label:"C"},{id:"d",text:"Oxidation of glucose into lactic acid in guard cells",label:"D"}],question:"Which of the following events is NOT part of the raw material processing during photosynthesis in green plants?",correct_id:"d",difficulty:"easy",category_id:"general-science",explanation:"Photosynthesis involves three steps: absorption of solar energy by chlorophyll, photolysis of water into $\\text{H}_2$ and $\\text{O}_2$, and reduction of $\\text{CO}_2$ to glucose. Oxidation of glucose to lactic acid occurs during anaerobic respiration in animal muscles, not photosynthesis."},{pyq:"JKSSB FAA 2024",tags:["Life Processes"],source:"Subi Malik",status:"published",options:[{id:"a",text:"Ethanol and Carbon Dioxide produced via aerobic respiration",label:"A"},{id:"b",text:"Lactic Acid produced via anaerobic breakdown of pyruvate in muscle cells",label:"B"},{id:"c",text:"Pyruvic Acid produced via complete oxidation in mitochondria",label:"C"},{id:"d",text:"Acetic Acid produced via bacterial fermentation in capillaries",label:"D"}],question:"During heavy physical exercise, severe cramps and muscle fatigue occur in human limbs due to the accumulation of which compound?",correct_id:"b",difficulty:"easy",category_id:"general-science",explanation:"When oxygen supply is insufficient during heavy exercise, 3-carbon pyruvate is converted into 3-carbon Lactic Acid in human muscle cytoplasm (anaerobic pathway). The accumulation of lactic acid causes muscle cramps."},{pyq:null,tags:["Life Processes"],source:"Subi Malik",status:"published",options:[{id:"a",text:"1 and 2 only",label:"A"},{id:"b",text:"2 and 3 only",label:"B"},{id:"c",text:"1 and 3 only",label:"C"},{id:"d",text:"1, 2, and 3",label:"D"}],question:"Consider the following statements regarding Human Blood Circulation:<br>1. Deoxygenated blood from the body enters the Right Atrium and is pumped to the lungs by the Right Ventricle via Pulmonary Artery.<br>2. Oxygenated blood returns from the lungs to the Left Atrium via Pulmonary Veins.<br>3. Pulmonary Artery is the only artery in the human body that carries oxygenated blood under high pressure.<br>Which of the statements given above are correct?",correct_id:"a",difficulty:"medium",category_id:"general-science",explanation:"Statements 1 and 2 accurately describe double circulation. Statement 3 is incorrect because the Pulmonary Artery carries deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle to the lungs; all other systemic arteries carry oxygenated blood."},{pyq:null,tags:["Life Processes"],source:"Subi Malik",status:"published",options:[{id:"a",text:"Neuron",label:"A"},{id:"b",text:"Nephron",label:"B"},{id:"c",text:"Alveolus",label:"C"},{id:"d",text:"Bowman's Capsule only",label:"D"}],question:"The microscopic structural and functional filtration unit of the human kidney is known as the:",correct_id:"b",difficulty:"easy",category_id:"general-science",explanation:"The Nephron is the structural and functional filtration unit of the kidney, consisting of the glomerulus, Bowman's capsule, and renal tubules. Neurons belong to the nervous system, and alveoli are respiratory air sacs in lungs."},{pyq:"JKSSB FAA 2024",tags:["Communicable Diseases"],source:"Subi Malik",status:"published",options:[{id:"a",text:"Tuberculosis and Cholera",label:"A"},{id:"b",text:"Poliomyelitis and Dengue",label:"B"},{id:"c",text:"Malaria and Kala-azar",label:"C"},{id:"d",text:"Influenza and Hepatitis B",label:"D"}],question:"Which of the following pairs of diseases is caused entirely by Bacterial Pathogens?",correct_id:"a",difficulty:"medium",category_id:"general-science",explanation:"Tuberculosis (Mycobacterium tuberculosis) and Cholera (Vibrio cholerae) are bacterial diseases. Poliomyelitis, Dengue, Influenza, and Hepatitis B are viral. Malaria and Kala-azar are protozoan diseases."},{pyq:null,tags:["Communicable Diseases"],source:"Subi Malik",status:"published",options:[{id:"a",text:"p-1, q-2, r-3",label:"A"},{id:"b",text:"p-2, q-1, r-3",label:"B"},{id:"c",text:"p-1, q-3, r-2",label:"C"},{id:"d",text:"p-3, q-2, r-1",label:"D"}],question:"Match the disease in Column-I with its causal protozoan parasite/vector in Column-II:<br>Column-I:<br>p. Malaria<br>q. Kala-azar (Visceral Leishmaniasis)<br>r. Sleeping Sickness<br>Column-II:<br>1. Plasmodium (Vector: Female Anopheles mosquito)<br>2. Leishmania donovani (Vector: Sandfly)<br>3. Trypanosoma (Vector: Tsetse fly)<br>Select the correct code:",correct_id:"a",difficulty:"medium",category_id:"general-science",explanation:"Malaria is caused by Plasmodium transmitted by female Anopheles. Kala-azar is caused by Leishmania transmitted by sandflies. Sleeping sickness is caused by Trypanosoma transmitted by tsetse flies."},{pyq:null,tags:["Communicable Diseases"],source:"Subi Malik",status:"published",options:[{id:"a",text:"1 and 3 only",label:"A"},{id:"b",text:"2, 3, and 4 only",label:"B"},{id:"c",text:"1, 2, and 3 only",label:"C"},{id:"d",text:"1, 2, 3, and 4 (All are correct)",label:"D"}],question:"Consider the following pairings of Vitamins / Minerals with their deficiency diseases:<br>1. Vitamin A $\\rightarrow$ Night Blindness (Nyctalopia)<br>2. Vitamin B1 (Thiamine) $\\rightarrow$ Beriberi<br>3. Vitamin C (Ascorbic Acid) $\\rightarrow$ Scurvy (Bleeding gums)<br>4. Vitamin D / Calcium $\\rightarrow$ Rickets in children<br>Which of the pairings given above are correct?",correct_id:"d",difficulty:"easy",category_id:"general-science",explanation:"All four pairings are classical NCERT deficiency facts: Vitamin A causes night blindness, Vitamin B1 causes beriberi, Vitamin C causes scurvy, and Vitamin D causes rickets."},{pyq:null,tags:["Communicable Diseases"],source:"Subi Malik",status:"published",options:[{id:"a",text:"Diphtheria",label:"A"},{id:"b",text:"Tuberculosis",label:"B"},{id:"c",text:"Typhoid",label:"C"},{id:"d",text:"Tetanus",label:"D"}],question:"The BCG Vaccine (Bacillus Calmette-Guérin) is administered worldwide for immunization against which life-threatening disease?",correct_id:"b",difficulty:"easy",category_id:"general-science",explanation:"BCG is an attenuated live bacterial vaccine used primarily against Tuberculosis (TB). DPT vaccine immunizes against Diphtheria, Pertussis (whooping cough), and Tetanus."},{pyq:"JKSSB FAA 2024",tags:["Ecosystem"],source:"Subi Malik",status:"published",options:[{id:"a",text:"Only 10% of total solar energy is captured by green plants during photosynthesis.",label:"A"},{id:"b",text:"Only 10% of the energy stored at one trophic level is transferred to the next higher trophic level as organic biomass.",label:"B"},{id:"c",text:"90% of energy is transferred to higher trophic levels while 10% is lost as heat to the environment.",label:"C"},{id:"d",text:"Energy flow in an ecosystem is multidirectional, maintaining a 10% balance across all decomposers.",label:"D"}],question:"According to Raymond Lindeman's 10% Law of energy transfer in an ecosystem:",correct_id:"b",difficulty:"medium",category_id:"general-science",explanation:"Lindeman's 10% Law states that on average, only 10% of organic energy available at a trophic level is incorporated into biomass at the next trophic level; 90% is lost during metabolic processes and as heat. Energy flow is strictly unidirectional."},{pyq:null,tags:["Ecosystem"],source:"Subi Malik",status:"published",options:[{id:"a",text:"1 and 2 only",label:"A"},{id:"b",text:"2 and 3 only",label:"B"},{id:"c",text:"1 and 3 only",label:"C"},{id:"d",text:"1, 2, and 3",label:"D"}],question:"Consider the following statements regarding Biological Magnification (Biomagnification):<br>1. Non-biodegradable chemicals like DDT and heavy metals accumulate progressively at higher trophic levels in a food chain.<br>2. Top carnivores (human beings, birds of prey) exhibit the highest concentration of toxic chemicals in their bodies.<br>3. Biodegradable organic pollutants undergo maximum biomagnification in aquatic food webs.<br>Which of the statements given above are correct?",correct_id:"a",difficulty:"medium",category_id:"general-science",explanation:"Statements 1 and 2 are correct. Statement 3 is incorrect because only non-biodegradable substances (which cannot be metabolized or excreted by organisms) undergo biomagnification; biodegradable organic waste is broken down by decomposers."},{pyq:"JKSSB FAA 2024",tags:["Ecosystem"],source:"Subi Malik",status:"published",options:[{id:"a",text:"1 and 2 only",label:"A"},{id:"b",text:"2 and 3 only",label:"B"},{id:"c",text:"1 and 3 only",label:"C"},{id:"d",text:"1, 2, and 3",label:"D"}],question:"Consider the following statements regarding the Stratospheric Ozone Layer:<br>1. Ozone ($\\text{O}_3$) in the stratosphere absorbs harmful ultraviolet-B (UV-B) radiation from the sun, protecting organisms from skin cancer and eye cataracts.<br>2. Ozone depletion is caused mainly by Chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs), which release active chlorine radicals upon exposure to UV rays.<br>3. The thickness of the ozone layer is measured in Dobson Units (DU).<br>Which of the statements given above are correct?",correct_id:"d",difficulty:"medium",category_id:"general-science",explanation:"All three statements are correct. Synthetic CFCs release free chlorine atoms in the stratosphere that catalyze the destruction of ozone molecules. Ozone layer thickness is expressed in Dobson Units (1 DU = 0.01 mm at standard temperature and pressure)."},{pyq:"JKSSB FAA 2024",tags:["Sources Of Energy"],source:"Subi Malik",status:"published",options:[{id:"a",text:"1 and 2 only",label:"A"},{id:"b",text:"2 and 3 only",label:"B"},{id:"c",text:"1 and 3 only",label:"C"},{id:"d",text:"1, 2, and 3",label:"D"}],question:"Consider the following statements regarding Biogas and Solar Energy:<br>1. Biogas is produced by the anaerobic decomposition of organic waste (cow dung, crop residue) and contains up to 75% Methane gas ($\\text{CH}_4$).<br>2. The primary semiconductor material used in fabricating commercial Solar Photovoltaic Cells is high-purity Silicon.<br>3. Biogas leaves no residue after burning and burns without smoke, having a high heating value.<br>Which of the statements given above are correct?",correct_id:"d",difficulty:"medium",category_id:"general-science",explanation:"Biogas consists of up to 75% methane, burns cleanly without smoke, and leaves nutrient-rich slurry used as manure. Solar PV cells utilize semiconductor Silicon to convert solar photons directly into electricity. All three statements are correct."}],l="2026-08-28T13:11:26.778325+00:00",b={id:e,exam_id:t,title:i,total_questions:30,total_marks:30,duration_mins:20,negative_marking:.25,window_start:a,window_end:o,result_reveal_at:n,status:r,questions_data:s,created_at:l};export{l as created_at,b as default,u as duration_mins,t as exam_id,e as id,h as negative_marking,s as questions_data,n as result_reveal_at,r as status,i as title,d as total_marks,c as total_questions,o as window_end,a as window_start};
